
  I lean TypeScript, only because the notification layer ends up with a browser client and a server pushing to it, and
  sharing the event types across that boundary is a genuine saving.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What stack should the server and frontend use? → TypeScript (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more before I draft the design: identity. Change events need an actor — "who changed it" is the core of a
  notification — so spec #1 has to say [3msomething[0m about users, but it doesn't have to ship authentication.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether spec #1 builds real auth or just the user concept that events attribute to.

  - [1mUser records now, real auth later.[0m A [38;5;153musers[39m table, an actor on every event, and a development-only "acting as"
    switcher so multi-user behaviour is testable immediately. Buys the ability to actually exercise "someone else
    changed your task" from day one, at a fraction of auth's cost. Costs: it is not secure and must never be deployed
    publicly as-is — the spec would say so explicitly, and auth gets its own spec before any real deployment.
  - [1mReal auth in spec #1.[0m Sessions, password hashing or a magic-link flow, the whole thing. Buys a deployable app at the
    end of spec #1. Costs: auth is a meaty subsystem with its own security surface, and it would roughly double spec #1
    while teaching us nothing about tasks or events.
  - [1mNo users at all yet.[0m Tasks with no actor. Cheapest, but it guts the event log — an event with no "by whom" can't
    produce a notification, which is the one thing we agreed to get right now.

  I recommend the first: it keeps spec #1 about tasks and events while making the multi-user behaviour real enough to
  build notifications against, and it defers auth to a spec that can take it seriously.
